    "metric_justification": "Committed noise-free multistart recovery for the naive family is 8, 7, 7, 7 for Ns = 1, 2, 3, 5: flat to slightly declining. Total squared separation rises monotonically over the same budgets, so it plainly does not predict recovery. The per-scalar metric is flat then declining, which is at least consistent with the observed rates. With four budgets and three tied recovery values this cannot establish that the per-scalar metric is predictive -- it only rules the total view out as a proxy for identifiability in this problem.",
    "headline_depends_on_metric": {
      "per_scalar": "Ns=2 optimized (0.122931) exceeds Ns=5 naive (0.058272) and Ns=8 naive (0.056602); even Ns=1 optimized (0.095964) exceeds both.",
      "total": "Ns=2 optimized (2.4586) does NOT exceed Ns=5 naive (2.9136) or Ns=8 naive (4.5282). On raw basin depth, more probes still win.",
      "honest_statement": "Optimized placement dominates naive placement at every budget on both views. The stronger claim -- that two optimized probes beat five or eight naive ones -- holds per measurement, not in raw basin depth."
